  "title": "Proposed: Tag-Based Environment Promotion (GitFlow)",
  "type": "page",
  "created": "",
  "updated": "",
  "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/335216656",
  "space": {
    "key": "SFCC",
    "name": "Adobe SFCC"
  },
  "version": 1,
  "attachments": [],
  "content": {
    "value": "**Draft proposal** — for review by squad leads + Integration lead before this replaces anything in AGENTS.md or the existing Branching Guide. Prepared by Claude Code, 2026-07-16, on `chore/read-me-cicd-updates`.\n\nThe problem with today's model\n------------------------------\n\nOnly **Dev** is continuously tied to `develop` (deploys on every merge) — that part is fine, it's meant to be the shared integration environment. But SIT/UAT/Stage/Prod are promoted by pointing a workflow at a **branch or tag ref** (`workflow_dispatch` with a `ref` input). In practice this breaks down two ways:\n\n1. **PR branches don't survive.** Once a story's PR merges to `develop`, its branch is typically deleted. There's nothing left to point a later deploy at — you'd have to keep branches alive indefinitely just to remember what shipped where, which doesn't scale past a couple of people.\n2. **Environments get forced into lockstep.** If Test, SIT, and UAT all deploy the *same* release branch as it exists right now, they can't run independent testing cycles — a fix landed for UAT is *also* now sitting on whatever branch Test/SIT were pointed at, even if those teams haven't finished their own pass yet.\n\nThe fix: promote immutable tags, not floating branches\n------------------------------------------------------\n\nThis is standard GitFlow practice that's currently missing here: **when a batch of work is ready to test, tag it. Every environment deploys a specific tag, never \"whatever's on a branch right now.\"**\n\n* Tags are **immutable and permanent** — `v1.4.0-rc1` always means exactly one commit, unlike a PR branch that gets deleted on merge.\n* Each environment can sit on a **different tag at the same time** — Test can be validating `v1.4.0-rc1` while SIT is still working through `v1.3.0-rc3` and UAT is signing off `v1.3.0-rc2`. That's what actually decouples the environments' cadences from each other.\n* **No new tooling required.** Every `deploy-*.yml` workflow already takes a `ref` input via `workflow_dispatch`, and `git checkout` doesn't care whether `ref` is a branch name or a tag name. The gap today is process (nobody's cutting tags), not infrastructure.\n\nFlow\n----\n\ntext\ndevelop ──● story A merges\n●\n● story B merges ─┐\n● │ batch ready → cut release/1.4.0\n●───────────────────release/1.4.0\n│\n● tag v1.4.0-rc1 ──▶ deploy to Test (early isolated look, this batch only)\n│\n(Test signs off, or a fix/release-1.4.0-\\* PR lands a fix)\n│\n● tag v1.4.0-rc2 (if a fix landed) ──▶ deploy to SIT\n│\n(SIT signs off, on NewStore's own cadence — doesn't block on UAT/Stage)\n│\n● tag v1.4.0 (final candidate) ──▶ UAT\n│\n(Business sign-off)\n│\n● same tag v1.4.0 ──▶ Stage (booked 2-week E2E window)\n│\n(Contractual perf/E2E sign-off)\n│\nmerge release/1.4.0 → main, tag v1.4.0 on main too,\nback-merge → develop (per existing GitFlow rules)\n│\n▼\nProd (release manager + canary gate)\n\nPer-environment example\n-----------------------\n\n| Environment | What it deploys today | What it would deploy under this proposal | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Local** | `npm start` against your working tree | *(unchanged)* | — |\n| **Dev** | Every merge to `develop` | *(unchanged — this is correct as-is, it's meant to be the noisy shared integration tier)* | Auto-deploys on every `develop` merge |\n| **Test** *(new — `deploy-test.yml`)* | N/A — didn't exist | A feature/fix branch, or an early `-rc` tag, deployed on demand for isolated pre-SIT review | `workflow_dispatch` → `ref: v1.4.0-rc1`, or even a raw feature branch for a quick look before a release is cut at all |\n| **SIT** | Whatever's on the `release/**` branch when the SIT Gate workflow completes | A specific `-rc` tag, re-deployed only when a new `-rc` is cut | `workflow_dispatch` → `ref: v1.4.0-rc2` |\n| **UAT** | Manual, arbitrary branch/tag | The release's final candidate tag | `workflow_dispatch` → `ref: v1.4.0` |\n| **Stage** | Manual, arbitrary branch/tag | Same tag UAT is validating, once UAT signs off | `workflow_dispatch` → `ref: v1.4.0` |\n| **Prod** | Manual, arbitrary branch/tag | The tag that passed Stage, pushed to `main` | `workflow_dispatch` → `ref: v1.4.0` |\n\nWhat changes vs. today\n----------------------\n\n|  | Today | Proposed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What gets deployed | A branch name, or an ad-hoc ref someone remembers | A named, immutable tag |\n| Tracking \"what's where\" | Tribal knowledge / Slack history | `git tag -l` / GitHub Releases page |\n| Can Test/SIT/UAT run independent cycles? | No — pointed at the same moving branch | Yes — each on its own tag |\n| New infrastructure needed | — | None — `deploy-test.yml` already added; existing `ref` inputs already accept tags |\n| New process needed | — | A tagging convention (below) + who's authorized to cut a tag |\n\nOpen questions for the team\n---------------------------\n\n1. **Tag naming convention** — `vX.Y.Z-rc1`, `vX.Y.Z-rc2`, ... final `vX.Y.Z`? Or date-based (`2026.07.16-rc1`)?\n2. **Who cuts tags?** Release manager only, or can any tier-1-approver do it?\n3. **Release-branch cut cadence** — currently roughly per-sprint. Cutting smaller/more frequent `release/**` branches (and therefore `-rc` tags) would get SIT fresher, still-isolated snapshots sooner, independent of this proposal but worth deciding alongside it.\n4. **Does `Test` need its own SIT-Gate-style CI check**, or is it purely a manual, no-gate \"quick look\" tier?",
    "format": "markdown"
  }
}"}

