    {
      "id": "18141",
      "key": "SFCC-96",
      "summary": "Navigation Menu — Desktop Mega Menu",
      "description": "As a\n Shopper,\nI want\n to see a mega menu appear when I hover over or click a top-level navigation category on desktop,\nso that\n I can quickly browse and navigate to subcategories and destinations without leaving the current page.\nContext:\nThe global header (SFCC-52) displays a row of top-level navigation categories. On desktop, hovering over a category triggers a mega menu dropdown that displays the associated links and subcategories for that heading. The mega menu should feel immediate and responsive — it is the primary navigation mechanism for desktop shoppers.\nKey behaviours:\nHovering over a top-level nav item reveals the mega menu for that category\nThe mega menu displays the subcategory links and any associated content (e.g. campaign imagery, featured links) as defined in the CMS\nThe mega menu dismisses when the shopper moves the cursor away from both the nav item and the open menu panel\nZ.Immerse is an exception — it functions as a direct link to a page and does not trigger a mega menu\nThe 'Back in Boutique' category is included in the nav and links to the relevant page without a mega menu\nNav items and their associated mega menu content are managed via the CMS; adding or removing items does not require a code change\nThe global navigation structure is cloneable across markets to support regional storefronts\nThis story is design-dependent — the layout, column structure, and any editorial content slots within the mega menu must come from the Figma spec before implementation begins.",
